 2. 🪖 &quot;Shark Tank&quot; for military bases 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Pete Hegseth gives a State of the Force address at Marine Corps Base Quantico. Photo: Alex Wong/Getty Images In a sweeping hourlong address to hundreds of junior officers and enlisted leaders at Quantico, Va., Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth proposed a pitch-off between states to see who should host America's newest military base, Axios' Thomas Novelly writes. 

 "If your state wants the honor of hosting 15- to 25,000 of America's great troops, show us why and how you're going to do it," Hegseth said. The big picture: New military infrastructure is a major economic driver for states and can lead to political tugs-of-war, like the yearslong fight between Colorado and Alabama to claim U.S. Space Command's headquarters. 

 America's newest military installation is Colorado's Schriever Space Force Base, which was opened in 1985. More details . 

 Palmer Luckey, Newt Gingrich and Elon Musk at Sec. Hegseth's Quantico address yesterday. Screenshot: DOD/via X Hegseth also announced " Project Meridian ," a Pentagon study of the future of warfare led by Elon Musk, Palmer Luckey and Newt Gingrich, Axios' Colin Demarest writes. 

 It's Musk's first official role advising the Trump administration since his very public feud last year with President Trump after departing DOGE. Meridian, Hegseth said, will examine "the domains we must conquer, and capabilities we must master." 

